{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794130336,"nonce":"wOHj9M/Vlybw4GT1avXhURkEdt0=","title":"CSS font-family keeps one name and one generic, so a font stack is not tried in order like a browser","message":"Found while settling the Linux default font list (d0af0bc). parse_font_family reduces the list to the first named family and the first generic keyword, and StyleFontFamily, the layout FontFamily, TextLayoutOptions and the measure context carry one name. The registry resolves that name, then the generic, then sans-serif. Every other listed family is dropped: font-family: \"Inter\", \"Helvetica Neue\", Arial, sans-serif looks for Inter and, if it is missing, goes straight to the generic sans. The theme stacks (--font-sans and the rest) are also reduced to their first name, so the fallbacks a theme lists do nothing.\n\nBrowsers walk the list in order and use the first installed family, then fall back per glyph. Blinc's own rule is to follow web standards, and a font stack is the standard way to ask for a face across platforms.\n\nNeeded:\n- StyleFontFamily and the layout FontFamily carry the ordered names plus the generic.\n- The registry resolves the names in order before the generic; measure, draw and the editable fields' TextFace resolve the same stack, so a box is sized in the face it is drawn in.\n- The measure cache key includes the whole stack.\n- Generic keywords end the named list as in CSS; system-ui keeps its platform meaning.\n- Tests: a stack whose first families are missing resolves to the first installed one, in measurement and in drawing; the theme stacks resolve through the CSS path.\n\nTouches blinc_css (parser, schema), blinc_layout (FontFamily, text build, measure contexts, the field TextFace), blinc_text (registry lookup) and the cache keys. Related: d0af0bc, 9d24660.","files":null}]}